Below I give a detailed, engineering-grade comparison (thermal, electrical, mechanical, EMI), practical manufacturing notes, mitigation strategies for the usual pitfalls (eddy/current loops, centrifugal stress), quick back-of-the-envelope calculations so you can compare copper area & DC resistance, suggested experiments to validate, and a clear recommendation for the 1x1 QMD motor path forward.

Short summary / headline recommendation
For the 1x1 QMD motor, flat ribbon / copper tape (insulated foil stacks, possibly multiple thin ribbons laminated) is likely the best practical choice for stator/drive coils: it gives higher slot fill, better surface area for cooling, lower DC resistance for a given package, and simpler manufacturing than heavy-copper PCB coils. Use thin, insulated ribbons (or stacked insulated foil) arranged to break circulating eddy loops and mechanically retain them (impregnation + banding). Reserve PCB coils only for very small, low-power prototypes or where precise planar geometry is needed and cost/weight are acceptable.

1 — Electrical performance (DC and AC)
    • DC resistance depends on conductor cross-sectional area (A) and resistivity ρ: R = ρ·L/A. A ribbon with the same area as a round wire has the same DC resistance. But the slot fill matters: ribbons pack flatter and reduce wasted slot space, so for the same slot volume you can have more copper cross-section, therefore lower R than using round conductors that leave gaps.
    • Surface area: ribbon has larger external surface area per unit copper — better convective cooling and easier conduction into graphoil/TIM.
    • Skin & proximity effects (AC): at electrical frequencies (f_elec = p·RPM/60), skin depth δ = sqrt(2ρ/ωμ). If coil currents are low frequency (EV motors: several hundred Hz at max electrical speed; e-bike low), skin/proximity can be significant for thick conductors.
        ◦ Mitigation: use many thin ribbons (stacked & insulated) rather than one thick foil. That reduces effective eddy currents and lowers AC loss (same principle as Litz wire).
        ◦ If you must use a single thick ribbon, consider transposing or splitting the ribbon into parallel insulated strips so induced circulating currents between layers are suppressed.
    • Conclusion: For the expected operating currents (tens to hundreds of A) and likely frequencies, thin insulated ribbon stacks or transposed foil stacks are preferred to avoid AC loss penalties.

2 — Thermal behavior & cooling
    • Ribbon increases external area contact to TIM/graphite spreader, enabling lower contact thermal resistance and faster heat extraction.
    • Higher fill factor → less copper resistance → lower I²R losses for same torque, or for same current gives higher torque — both help thermal budget.
    • Ribbon allows direct bonding to graphoil spreader or thermal pads across broad surfaces, lowering contact resistance vs round wires that only touch at discrete points.

3 — EMI, eddy currents and magnetic losses
    • Risk: wide, continuous foil can support large eddy currents in the presence of time-varying magnetic flux (especially if ribbon runs form large loops or are exposed to radial field variation).
    • Mitigations:
        1. Segment / insulate between layers — stack N thin insulated ribbons instead of one thick piece. Breaking vertical conduction suppresses loop currents between layers.
        2. Transposition — if using several parallel ribbons, interchange their relative positions along the winding (or use braided/stranded ribbon) to average out induced voltages.
        3. Slot liners / magnetic shielding — use insulating ferromagnetic liners or skew slots to smooth flux coupling and reduce concentrated crossing flux.
        4. Use rectangular bars with insulated gaps for very high current, but treat as many parallel ribbons.
        5. If high-frequency switching/fast transients are present, use snubbers, proper PWM filtering, and EMI shielding to control emissions.
    • PCB coils: inherently segmented (traces separated by dielectric) so they avoid internal large eddy loops, but individual trace thickness is small so DC R is high unless heavy copper used (costly).

4 — Mechanical & manufacturing considerations
    • Winding: ribbon can be wound, but must be controlled to prevent twisting or sharp corners. Use mandrels / formers shaped to the slot.
    • Insulation: prefer pre-insulated flat copper tape or apply thin enamel/phosphor-bronze adhesive + Kapton/nomex. Keep insulation thin to keep fill factor high.
    • Joining: soldering many ribbon layers is harder than soldering round wire. Use ultrasonic welding, resistance spot welding, or brazing for high-amp joints; mechanical crimps with large contact areas can work if properly plated. For series connections between ribbon runs, consider laser or ultrasonic welding.
    • Centrifugal forces (rotor coils): if coils are on the rotor, ribbons must be retained against radial outward force. Use high-strength banding, potting compounds, or mechanical clamps. If stator winding, less severe.
    • Vibration & abrasion: encapsulate with varnish or epoxy and use mechanical anchors at turn ends.
    • Thermal expansion & cycling: laminates & adhesives must tolerate cycles; graphoil contact requires compliant TIM to maintain low contact resistance.

5 — Practical construction options & patterns
    1. Stacked, insulated foil ribbons: e.g., 5 × (0.25 mm × 10 mm) insulated ribbons stacked — gives low R, manageable skin depth, simple stacking. Use polymer interleaves for insulation.
    2. Interleaved pancake coils using ribbons (good for axial flux or planar motors).
    3. Rectangular copper bars / busbars for very low-turn, high-current windings (use varnish/impregnation and segmentation to reduce eddy loops).
    4. Litz-like flat wire: many insulated micro-ribbons twisted/transposed — combines skin mitigation with good fill.
    5. PCB (heavy copper) only if: small size, low power, need for integrated sensors/embedded routes, or rapid prototyping — otherwise heavy copper PCBs are heavy and expensive for high current.

6 — Quick numerical example (compare effective copper area and DC resistance)
Example: round wire d = 2.0 mm → A_round = π·(2.0)^2/4 = 3.1416 mm^2.
Ribbon: w = 4.0 mm, t = 0.9 mm → A_ribbon = 4.0×0.9 = 3.6 mm^2 → ~15% more area → ~15% lower DC resistance for same conductor length.
If slot allows 4 round wires (4×3.1416 = 12.566 mm^2) vs 3 ribbon stacks (3×3.6 = 10.8 mm^2) — this depends on packing, but generally ribbon increases usable copper fraction because packing is tighter (less interstitial air). Always calculate slot fill = copper_area_total / slot_volume_cross_section.

7 — How ribbon helps copper losses & heat simultaneously
    • Higher fill factor → lower copper resistance and lower I²R losses.
    • Larger exposed area per conductor → better convective cooling and easier conduction into graphoil spreader → lower operating temperature and thus lower resistivity increase with temperature.
    • These two effects are multiplicative: better fill reduces loss, improved cooling reduces temperature rise, reducing resistivity and allowing higher current duty.

8 — Downsides & where PCB might still win
    • Eddy currents: poor ribbon implementation can be worse than round wire if large area loops form. Must engineer layers/insulation.
    • Mechanical complexity: making many fine, insulated ribbons and assembling them can be more labor-intensive than winding round wire on a coil winder (but this is easily automated).
    • Joining: ribbon joints require welding/crimping; soldering thick ribbons can be tricky.
    • PCB advantages: precision geometry, integrated features (sensors, connectors), and easy repeatability for low power. But PCB copper thickness limits and cost for very heavy copper make them less attractive for the high-current motors you’re exploring.

9 — EMI & thermal synergy — design pattern I recommend
For your 1x1 QMD motor:
    1. Use stacked insulated ribbon packs for each slot; each pack is composed of N thin foil ribbons (e.g., 5–12 ribbons of 0.2–0.5 mm thickness), each individually insulated. This reduces eddy loops and skin loss.
    2. Bond the outer face of the ribbon pack to a graphoil layer with a thin compliant TIM to maximize spreading to fins. Ensure graphoil sectors are electrically isolated to avoid eddy loops.
    3. Consider 2–4 thin flat heat pipes routed to the fin pack if radial route is long (large rotor diameters). Keep them minimal — the graphoil carries lateral heat, pipes carry hub→rim.
    4. Impregnate/varnish the winding for mechanical integrity and to reduce micro-movements which can cause wear and EMI.
    5. Use mechanical retaining bands and/or resin potting at the coil edges to survive centrifugal loads.
    6. For connectors/joints, plan for ultrasonic welds or brazed joints rather than simple solder for high current.

10 — Tests & validation plan (practical)
Before committing to mass manufacturing:
    1. Build 3 small prototype winding samples:
        ◦ A: round wire baseline
        ◦ B: ribbon stack (as-built)
        ◦ C: PCB coil (if feasible)
    2. Measure DC resistance & IR thermal test at target current (20 A / 100 A pulses) and record temperature vs time.
    3. Measure AC loss at switching frequency of your controller (or simulated waveform) to capture skin/proximity losses.
    4. Centrifuge or spin test rotor-mounted sample at expected RPM to validate mechanical retention.
    5. EMI scan and near-field measurement with and without graphoil shielding.
    6. Thermal characterization: measure effective R_th from coil to ambient for each cooling approach (graphoil alone, graphoil + few heat pipes, heat-pipe only).
    7. Use measured R_th and losses to update surrogate & Pareto.

11 — Additional manufacturing & supply notes
    • Pre-insulated copper foil is available from magnet wire suppliers (Polyimide/Kapton coated foils). Specify thickness and coating class.
    • For large-scale runs, stamping preformed ribbon coils or automated ribbon winders reduce labor.
    • Consider contacting coil manufacturers experienced in foil windings (servo/traction motors use similar tech) for production guidance.